cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use HerdBench, please cite it using the metadata in this file."
type: software
title: "HerdBench: grading public crowding signals against observed positions"
abstract: >-
  HerdBench tests whether public crowding and positioning signals (funding,
  premium, open interest, momentum, comovement, liquidations, Binance
  long/short ratios and equity short interest) track how traders are actually
  positioned. The truth comes from daily snapshots of every open position on
  Hyperliquid, a derivatives venue where all positions are public. Sub-accounts
  are merged into their master account, market makers and protocol accounts are
  removed, and net positioning and crowding metrics are computed for each
  snapshot date, market and cohort. Every registered parameter was fixed and
  timestamped before any signal was compared with the truth. The package
  contains the truth pipeline, the proxies, the grading statistics and integrity
  gates, a command-line interface, and a function that grades any signal
  against the published, disclosure-controlled truth panel.
authors:
